== Batteries
An electric battery converts chemical energy into electrical energy using electrochemical cells with a positive terminal (cathode), negative terminal (anode), and an electrolyte.
- *Primary Battery (Disposable):* Used once and discarded; non-rechargeable (e.g., alkaline batteries).
- *Secondary Battery (Rechargeable):* Can be charged and used multiple times by reversing current (e.g., Li-ion, lead-acid).
- *C-Rate:* Defined as $"Discharge Current" / "Battery Capacity"$.


=== Difference Between Lead-Acid Battery and Li-ion Battery
- *Lead-Acid Battery:* Old technology using lead/lead dioxide; low energy density (25–45 Wh/kg); lower efficiency (60–95%); cheaper; used in automobiles and backup systems.
- *Li-ion Battery:* Modern technology using lithium-based electrodes; high energy density (100–150 Wh/kg); higher efficiency (90–100%); more expensive; used in EVs and electronics.

=== Types of Batteries 
1. *Lead-Acid Battery:* Invented in 1859; uses sulfuric acid electrolyte; cheap and reliable for automobiles and UPS.
2. *Nickel Batteries:* Include Ni-Cd (long life, toxic cadmium, strong memory effect) and Ni-MH (improved version, used in hybrid vehicles).
3. *Lithium-ion Battery:* High energy density and efficiency; lightweight; expensive; used in EVs and mobile devices.
4. *Flow Battery:* Uses chemical reactions between two liquid electrolytes stored in separate tanks; very long cycle life; used for large-scale grid storage.
5. *Metal-Air Battery:* Uses metal electrode and oxygen (zinc-air/aluminium-air); high energy density (110–420 Wh/kg); low charging efficiency (~50%); used in small devices.
6. *Sodium-Sulphur (NaS) Battery:* High-temperature battery using molten sodium and sulfur; high energy density (150–240 Wh/kg); used for large-scale grid storage.

